.\" tmpfile.3 - create a temporary file
.TH TMPFILE 3 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
tmpfile, tmpnam \- create a temporary file
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "FILE *tmpfile(void);"
.PP
.BI "char *tmpnam(char *" s ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR tmpfile ()
function opens a unique temporary file in binary read/write
.RB ( "w+" )
mode.
The file is created beneath
.I /tmp
with a name of the form
.IR /tmp/.tmpf.<pid>.<counter> ,
opened with
.BR O_RDWR ,
.BR O_CREAT ,
and
.B O_EXCL
flags and mode 0600, and then immediately
.BR unlink (2)ed
so that the file is removed automatically when the returned stream is closed
or when the process exits.
Up to 100 candidate names are tried before failure is reported.
.PP
The
.BR tmpnam ()
function generates a file name string that is suitable for use as a
temporary file name, of the form
.IR /tmp/tmp.<pid>.<counter> .
If the argument
.I s
is not
.BR NULL ,
the name is written into the buffer pointed to by
.IR s ,
which must have room for at least
.B L_tmpnam
bytes; otherwise a pointer to a static buffer is returned and may be
overwritten by subsequent calls.
.SS "Security and deprecation of tmpnam()"
.BR tmpnam ()
is
.B deprecated
and should not be used in new code.
The function returns only a path
.I name
\(em it does not create or open the file.
Between the time
.BR tmpnam ()
returns and the time the caller opens the file there is a window in
which another process may create a file (or symbolic link) at the same
path, leading to a classic time-of-check / time-of-use
.RB ( TOCTOU )
race.
An attacker who wins this race can cause the calling program to
truncate, overwrite, or read an arbitrary file with the privileges of
the caller, which is a known vector for symlink attacks in setuid and
daemon programs.
.PP
New code should use
.BR tmpfile ()
\(em which performs the create-and-unlink atomically with
.B O_EXCL
\(em or
.BR mkstemp (3)
when a named file is required.
Both interfaces remove the race because the file is created by the
same call that returns the descriptor or stream.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR tmpfile ()
returns a pointer to the open stream.
On failure it returns
.B NULL
and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.PP
.BR tmpnam ()
returns a pointer to the name string.
If
.I s
is non-NULL the value returned is
.IR s ;
otherwise it is a pointer to an internal static buffer of length
.BR L_tmpnam .
On failure
.B NULL
is returned.
.SH ERRORS
.BR tmpfile ()
may fail and set
.I errno
for any of the reasons given for
.BR open (2),
.BR unlink (2),
or
.BR fdopen (3).
In particular:
.TP
.B EACCES
Search permission is denied for a directory in the path prefix of
.IR /tmp ,
or write permission is denied on
.IR /tmp .
.TP
.B EEXIST
A unique name could not be generated within the internal retry limit.
.TP
.B EMFILE
The per-process limit on open file descriptors has been reached.
.TP
.B ENFILE
The system-wide limit on the total number of open files has been reached.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
Insufficient memory was available to allocate the
.B FILE
object.
.TP
.B ENOSPC
The filesystem containing
.I /tmp
has no space for a new file.
.TP
.B EROFS
.I /tmp
resides on a read-only filesystem.
.PP
.BR tmpnam ()
itself performs no I/O and therefore reports no errors via
.IR errno .
A subsequent attempt to
.BR open (2)
the returned name may, of course, fail for any of the reasons listed in
.BR open (2).
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program creates a temporary file, writes to it, rewinds,
and reads the data back.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    FILE *fp = tmpfile();
    char buf[16];

    if (fp == NULL) {
        perror("tmpfile");
        return 1;
    }
    fputs("scratch", fp);
    rewind(fp);
    if (fgets(buf, sizeof(buf), fp) != NULL)
        printf("read back: %s\en", buf);
    fclose(fp);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkstemp (3),
.BR mktemp (3),
.BR fopen (3),
.BR fdopen (3),
.BR open (2),
.BR unlink (2),
.BR tmpfile (3)
.SH STANDARDS
.BR tmpfile ()
and
.BR tmpnam ()
conform to ISO C99 and POSIX.1-2008.
.BR tmpnam ()
is marked
.B obsolescent
in POSIX.1-2008 and is scheduled for removal in future revisions of the
standard; portable code should prefer
.BR mkstemp (3)
or
.BR tmpfile ().
